{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"The key observation is logical: each side of both `Iff`s is independently true. So do not try to derive the lattice-volume theorem from compactness, or finite generation from the fractional-part statement. Prove the four universal assertions separately, then assemble the two equivalences by ignoring the implication hypotheses.\n\nAll four facts come from the same coordinate/fundamental-parallelepiped picture:\n\n- Under a basis \\(b\\), the fundamental domain is the image of \\([0,1)^\\iota\\). Change of variables gives its volume as \\(|\\det(\\operatorname{Matrix.of} b)|\\); this is exactly `ZSpan.volume_real_fundamentalDomain`. [Mathlib source](https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Algebra/Module/ZLattice/Basic.lean#L330-L353)\n\n- For a continuous \\(L\\)-periodic map, choose a finite \\(\\mathbb Z\\)-basis of the lattice. Every vector is lattice-equivalent to a point in the associated closed parallelepiped, so the range is the continuous image of a compact set. This is `IsZLattice.isCompact_range_of_periodic`. The closed parallelepiped is important here because the half-open fundamental domain need not be compact. [Mathlib source](https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Algebra/Module/ZLattice/Basic.lean#L716-L730)\n\n- A discrete full lattice in a finite-dimensional proper space is finitely generated: choose a maximal linearly independent subset, represent quotient classes inside a bounded fundamental domain, and use that a bounded set meets a discrete subgroup in finitely many points. This yields `ZLattice.module_finite`. Properness is the essential compactness assumption in this step. [Mathlib source](https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Algebra/Module/ZLattice/Basic.lean#L397-L439)\n\n- Finally, `fract b x` has coordinates \\(a_i-\\lfloor a_i\\rfloor\\in[0,1)\\), hence lies in the fundamental domain; this is `ZSpan.fract_mem_fundamentalDomain`. [Mathlib source](https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/master/Mathlib/Algebra/Module/ZLattice/Basic.lean#L143-L176)\n\nAfter establishing these four goals, construct the outer conjunction and both `Iff`s from the unconditional conclusions."}}
